      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] confirms WAL mode is persistent across close/reopen cycles. Passage [0] states it is not possible to change page_size after entering WAL mode and that one must be in rollback journal mode to change page size. The combination of these facts supports the claim that persistence prevents simple reopening from allowing page size changes, requiring a switch to rollback mode.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Unlike the other journaling modes, PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL is persistent. If a process sets WAL mode, then closes and reopens the database, the database will come back in WAL mode."
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "It is not possible to change the page_size after entering WAL mode... You must be in a rollback journal mode to change the page size."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "From version 3.7.0 until 3.22.0, reading a WAL-mode database required write access.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] establishes WAL availability starting at 3.7.0. Passage [3] states older versions could not read WAL databases without write access and this constraint was relaxed beginning with 3.22.0. This implies the requirement existed from the introduction of WAL (3.7.0) up to 3.22.0.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "Beginning with version 3.7.0 (2010-07-21), a new \"Write-Ahead Log\" option (hereafter referred to as \"WAL\") is available."
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Older versions of SQLite could not read a WAL-mode database that was read-only. In other words, write access was required in order to read a WAL-mode database. This constraint was relaxed beginning with SQLite version 3.22.0 (2018-01-22)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "SQLite's default page size is 4096 bytes.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "None of the provided passages mention the specific value of the default page size.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Converting a database to WAL fails if the VFS does not support the necessary shared-memory primitives, in which case the journal_mode pragma returns an error.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] states that if conversion fails due to VFS limitations, the journaling mode remains unchanged and the pragma returns the *prior* journaling mode string (e.g., \"delete\"), not an error.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "If the conversion to WAL could not be completed (for example, if the VFS does not support the necessary shared-memory primitives) then the journaling mode will be unchanged and the string returned from the primitive will be the prior journaling mode (for example \"delete\")."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "Redis offers three appendfsync policies.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] lists `appendfsync always`, `appendfsync everysec`, and `appendfsync no`. Passage [2] also mentions 'different fsync policies: no fsync at all, fsync every second, fsync at every query'.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "* `appendfsync always`: ... * `appendfsync everysec`: ... * `appendfsync no`: ..."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "you can have different fsync policies: no fsync at all, fsync every second, fsync at every query."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With appendfsync always, Redis calls fsync every time new commands are appended to the AOF, which is very safe but very slow.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that `appendfsync always` performs `fsync` every time new commands are appended and describes it as 'Very very slow, very safe'.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "`appendfsync always`: `fsync` every time new commands are appended to the AOF. Very very slow, very safe."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The default policy is appendfsync no, which leaves flushing to the operating system; on Linux this normally means data is flushed every 30 seconds.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] explicitly states that the suggested (and default) policy is `fsync` every second (`appendfsync everysec`). While Passage [0] confirms that `appendfsync no` leaves flushing to the OS and Linux flushes every 30 seconds, the claim that this is the *default* policy is false.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "The suggested (and default) policy is to `fsync` every second."
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "`appendfsync no`: Never `fsync`, just put your data in the hands of the Operating System... Normally Linux will flush data every 30 seconds with this configuration"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With everysec you may lose about one second of data in a disaster.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states for `appendfsync everysec`: 'you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster.' Passage [2] reiterates: 'you can only lose one second worth of writes.'",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "`appendfsync everysec`: `fsync` every second... and you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "so you can only lose one second worth of writes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Because fsync runs on a background thread, the main thread never has to wait for disk I/O",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] states that 'fsync is performed using a background thread' and that the main thread tries hard to perform writes when no fsync is in progress. It does not state that the main thread *never* has to wait for disk I/O; in fact, Passage [0] notes that for `appendfsync always`, there is a single write and fsync before sending replies, implying synchronization. The absolute claim 'never has to wait' is not supported by the text.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "and appendfsync always typically cuts write throughput by more than half.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] describes `appendfsync always` as 'Very very slow', but provides no specific quantitative metric regarding throughput reduction (e.g., 'more than half'). This specific statistic is not present in the provided passages.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}

